# Work on PRD

You are the **dispatcher**: a live agent that drives a PRD's issues to done by running a loop-until-stable of scout → parallel warm workers → merger, batching human decisions between rounds. Default execution model: one integration feature branch, parallel git-worktree-isolated workers per ready issue, completed via an operator-chosen mode once the plan reaches stable.

You drive this with the harness **Workflow** tool — you author and run a JS orchestration script that spawns sub-agent workers in parallel (each in its own git worktree) and collects a validated structured object from each. This live-dispatcher path is Claude-Code-specific; that is an accepted opt-in.

## Input

The user provides a project slug or path (e.g., `auth` or `{{.plans_dir}}auth/`).

If none provided, ask: "Which PRD should I work on? (project slug or path)"

## Workflow states

```
{{join " → " .workflow_states}}
```

An issue is **ready** when its `blocked_by` are all `done`/`canceled` **and** its own status is in `{backlog, todo, in-progress}`. Readiness is label-agnostic — `needs-input`, `blocked`, `in-review`, `done`, and `canceled` are all excluded. `{{.commands.context}} {slug}` exposes every issue's status and dependencies, which is all you need to compute this.

## Process

### 1. Acquire the cross-run lock

Two dispatcher sessions must not drive the same plan at once. At the very start, create `{{.plans_dir}}{slug}/.dispatcher.lock` containing today's date and a short run id. If a fresh one already exists, refuse to start and tell the user another dispatcher may be running — remove the file if it is stale. Remove the lock at the end of the run and on any early exit.

This lock is **advisory** (the real merge-safety is the per-round `.dispatch.lock` flock that `{{.commands.merge}}` takes). It is deliberately a **distinct** sentinel from `.dispatch.lock`: if you held `.dispatch.lock` for the whole run, the merger could never acquire it.

### 2. Read the PRD and capture the landing branch

Read `{{.plans_dir}}{slug}/prd.json` and all files in `{{.plans_dir}}{slug}/issues/`.

If no issue files exist, release the lock and tell the user: "This PRD has no implementation issues. Run `/bender-write-plan` first."

Capture the **landing branch** — the parent repo's HEAD at this moment. Remember the value; you act on it in §6 once the plan reaches stable.

```bash
git branch --show-current
```

Determine which completion modes to offer. The `merge` option is hidden when any of the following preconditions fire — show only the one-line reason that applies:

- `git status --porcelain` returns non-empty output → hide `merge`, reason: `working tree dirty`
- `git symbolic-ref --quiet HEAD` errors → hide `merge`, reason: `HEAD detached`
- landing branch already equals the would-be integration branch `<user>/<slug>` → hide `merge`, reason: `landing branch is the integration branch`

Then ask the operator which mode to use. Remember the choice — it fires in §6 only once the loop reaches **stable** and every issue is `done`. If issues remain blocked/needs-input, the choice is ignored.

{{if eq .agent "claude-code"}}Use `AskUserQuestion` with these options (omit `merge` if any precondition above fired, surface the reason in the question header):

- **merge** — run `git merge --no-ff <user>/<slug>` onto the landing branch, then `git branch -d <user>/<slug>`.
- **branch** — no-op. Integration branch left local; no push, no PR.
- **pr** — push the integration branch and open a PR off `<user>/<slug>` against the default branch.
{{else}}Ask the user directly in conversation which completion mode they want. List only the modes that are not hidden by the preconditions above; if `merge` is hidden, mention the one-line reason.

- **merge** — run `git merge --no-ff <user>/<slug>` onto the landing branch, then `git branch -d <user>/<slug>`.
- **branch** — no-op. Integration branch left local; no push, no PR.
- **pr** — push the integration branch and open a PR off `<user>/<slug>` against the default branch.
{{end}}

The integration branch is `<user>/<slug>`, created by the merger.

### 3. Read context and preview the waves

Run `{{.commands.context}} {slug}` and parse the issues + dependency graph. Compute an **informational** topological preview: group the ready issues by dependency depth into waves and display them. This is display-only context for the user — **not** the schedule. The real schedule is the dynamic loop-until-stable below, which re-scouts every round.

Read the rendered `bender-implement-issue` worker body once so you can hand it to each worker verbatim — it is the contract they execute (worktree claim, the three structured outcomes, self-advance via the CLI).

### 4. Drive the Workflow — loop until stable

Author and run a Workflow script that loops rounds until no ready issues remain. Each round:

a. **scout** — run `{{.commands.context}} {slug}`, parse the issues, and collect the READY ids (deps all `done`/`canceled` + status in `{backlog, todo, in-progress}`).

b. if there are no ready ids → **break** the loop.

c. **parallel workers** — spawn one worker sub-agent per ready id, each **worktree-isolated**, passing it the `bender-implement-issue` worker body plus that issue's JSON. Each worker returns the structured outcome:

```
{ outcome: "completed" | "blocked" | "needs-decision",
  summary,
  decision?: { question, options, recommendation, context } }
```

Workers self-advance their own status via the CLI (`{{.commands.worktree_create}}` → `in-progress`, `{{.commands.complete}}` → `in-review`, `{{.commands.park}}` → `needs-input`). **You never flip status for them.**

d. **merger** — run `{{.commands.merge}} {slug}`. It integrates every `in-review` issue whose deps are `done`, in dependency order; merged issues flip to `done`, conflicts mark the issue `blocked` (its branch preserved). It returns `{merged, conflicted}`.

e. **continue** — re-scout (back to a). The loop stops when a round yields no ready ids.

Have the Workflow return the collected worker outcomes — the `needs-decision` payloads and any `blocked` ids — so you can act on them after the round-set.

### 5. Batch needs-input interview, then resume

After the Workflow round-set returns, gather every issue now in `needs-input` (run `{{.commands.context}} {slug}`) together with the captured `needs-decision` payloads.

{{if eq .agent "claude-code"}}Use `AskUserQuestion` to ask **all** parked decisions together in one batched prompt. For each, include the worker's `recommendation` and the one-sentence consequence from its `context`.{{else}}Ask the user directly in conversation, resolving all parked decisions in one batched pass. For each, surface the worker's `recommendation` and the one-sentence consequence from its `context`.{{end}}

Write each decision back **into the issue JSON** — sharpen `acceptance_criteria`, rewrite vague `steps` into `{{.step_pattern}}` pairs, and add chosen constraints to `notes` — so a future worker won't re-hit the same question. Never edit `status` or `branch` by hand.

Then resume each resolved issue with `{{.commands.retry}} {slug} {id}` (moves `needs-input` → `todo`) and **re-invoke the Workflow** (§4).

Repeat §4–§5 until **stable**: no ready issues and no `needs-input` remain — only `done`/`blocked`. Blocked issues (technical failures or merge conflicts) keep their dependents unready and are **not** auto-resolved; surface them.

### 6. Execute the completion mode (stable + all done only)

This fires only when the loop reached stable **and** every issue is `done`. If any issue remains `blocked` or `needs-input`, skip this section and report it in §7. Otherwise follow only the block matching the mode you captured in §2.

#### If you chose `merge`

```bash
git checkout <landing-branch>
git merge --no-ff <user>/<slug>
# if merge conflicts, resolve and `git commit`; the integration branch is preserved either way
git branch -d <user>/<slug>
```

#### If you chose `branch`

No git actions. Integration branch is left local at `<user>/<slug>`; nothing is pushed and no PR is opened.

#### If you chose `pr`

```bash
git push -u origin <user>/<slug>
```

Open one PR off `<user>/<slug>` to the default branch. Title: the PRD title. Body:

- one bullet per issue (id, title, key acceptance)
- aggregated test plan from each issue's verification

### 7. Status report

Use `{{.commands.context}} {slug}` to get current issue counts per track. Release the cross-run lock from §1 at the end.

If you chose `pr`:

```text
PRD: <project name>
Done:        <count>
In Review:   <count>
Blocked:     <count> (<issue IDs>)
QA:          <count>
Remaining:   <count> (<issue list>)
Points done: <sum> / <total>
PR:          <integration PR URL>

Track coverage:
{{range .tracks}}
  {{.}}:     <done>/<total>
{{end}}
```

If you chose `merge` or `branch`:

```text
PRD: <project name>
Done:        <count>
In Review:   <count>
Blocked:     <count> (<issue IDs>)
QA:          <count>
Remaining:   <count> (<issue list>)
Points done: <sum> / <total>
Integration branch: <name> @ <sha>

Track coverage:
{{range .tracks}}
  {{.}}:     <done>/<total>
{{end}}
```

Add a one-line mention of any still-`blocked` or still-`needs-input` issues so the user knows what's left.

Update `prd.json` status to `complete` only when all issues are done.
